\begin_layout Standard
# Basis transformations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## Bases
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Until now, we have been working with one specific coordinate system in which vectors are identified with their coordinate vectors with respect to the *standard basis vectors*
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\ve_1=\begin{pmatrix}1\\ 0\\ 0\\ \vdots\\ 0\end{pmatrix},\quad
\ve_2=\begin{pmatrix}0\\ 1\\ 0\\ \vdots\\ 0\end{pmatrix},\quad
\ldots,\quad
\ve_n=\begin{pmatrix}0\\ 0\\ 0\\ \vdots\\ 1\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In other words, a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\vv=\begin{pmatrix}v_1\\ v_2\\ \vdots\\
v_n\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset

 means the same as the corresponding linear combination of the standard basis vectors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\vv = v_1 \ve_1 + \cdots + v_n \ve_n.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In many situations, it is useful to express vectors in a different coordinate system. For this we need the general concept of a *basis*.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
:::
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
{
\end_layout

\end_inset

prf:definition
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Basis A *basis* of 
\begin_inset Formula $\RR^n$
\end_inset

 consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $(\vb_1,\ldots,\vb_n)$
\end_inset

 such that every vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\vv\in\RR^n$
\end_inset

 can be written in exactly one way as a *linear combination*
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\vv=c_1\vb_1+\cdots+c_n\vb_n
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
of 
\begin_inset Formula $\vb_1,\ldots,\vb_n$
\end_inset

 with scalars 
\begin_inset Formula $c_1,\ldots,c_n$
\end_inset

. These scalars are called the *coordinates* of 
\begin_inset Formula $\vv$
\end_inset

 relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\vb_1,\ldots,\vb_n)$
\end_inset

. The vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\begin{pmatrix}c_1\\ \vdots\\
c_n\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset

 is called the *coordinate vector* of 
\begin_inset Formula $\vv$
\end_inset

 relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\vb_1,\ldots,\vb_n)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
:::
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alternatively, a basis consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\vb_1,\ldots,\vb_n$
\end_inset

 with the following two properties:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- they *span* the space 
\begin_inset Formula $\RR^n$
\end_inset

, i.e. every vector is a linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $\vb_1,\ldots,\vb_n$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- they are *linearly independent*, i.e. the only way to write the zero vector as a linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $\vb_1,\ldots,\vb_n$
\end_inset

 is as 
\begin_inset Formula $0\vb_1+\cdots+0\vb_n$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
:::
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
{
\end_layout

\end_inset

note
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 A priori, we could have defined a basis as a collection of *some* number (say 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

) of vectors that span the space and are linearly independent. However, it can be shown that in fact any two bases contain the same number of vectors. Since the standard basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\RR^n$
\end_inset

 consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 vectors, the same therefore holds for *any* basis. :::
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## Basis transformation matrices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose we have two bases of 
\begin_inset Formula $\RR^n$
\end_inset

, say
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
B=(\vb_1, \ldots, \vb_n)\quad\text{and}\quad
C=(\vc_1, \ldots, \vc_n).
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
:::
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
{
\end_layout

\end_inset

