\end_layout

\end_inset

 Basis transformation matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The *basis transformation matrix* (or *change-of-basis matrix*) from 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P=P_{B\to C}$
\end_inset

 such that the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

-th column of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 contains the coordinates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\vb_j$
\end_inset

 relative to the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $(\vc_1,\ldots,\vc_n)$
\end_inset

. :::
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 means (note the difference with the formula 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
{
\end_layout

\end_inset

eq
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset

`matrix-vector` for matrix-vector multiplication)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\vb_j = \sum_{i=1}^n P_{i,j}\vc_i.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\vx$
\end_inset

, we write 
\begin_inset Formula $\vx^{(B)}=(x_1^{(B)},\ldots,x_n^{(B)})$
\end_inset

 for the coordinate vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $\vx$
\end_inset

 with respect to the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $B=(\vb_1,\ldots,\vb_n)$
\end_inset

, and we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\vx^{(C)}$
\end_inset

 similarly. Then we calculate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{aligned}
\vx &= \sum_{j=1}^n x_j^{(B)} \vb_j\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^n x_j^{(B)}\sum_{i=1}^n P_{i,j}\vc_i\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^n\left(\sum_{j=1}^n P_{i,j} x_j^{(B)}\right) \vc_i\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^n\bigl(P \vx^{(B)}\bigr)_i \vc_i,
\end{aligned}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which shows that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\vx^{(C)} = P_{B\to C}\vx^{(B)}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly, given a linear map 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, we write 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(B)}$
\end_inset

 for the matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 with respect to the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $B=(\vb_1,\ldots,\vb_n)$
\end_inset

, and likewise for 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(C)}$
\end_inset

. Then 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(B)}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(C)}$
\end_inset

 are related by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A^{(C)} = PA^{(B)}P^{-1}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Namely, consider what happens when we multiply this matrix to a coordinate vector of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\vx^{\vw}$
\end_inset

. First, applying 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{-1}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\vx^{\vw}$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\vx^{\vv}$
\end_inset

. Next, applying 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(B)}$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $(A\vx)^{(B)}$
\end_inset

. Finally, applying 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $(A\vx)^{(C)}$
\end_inset

, which is what we wanted to show.
\end_layout

